    "lawCode": "Amd §265.20, rpld §265.20 sub a ¶15, Pen L",
    "lawSection": "Penal Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S4922\n\nSPONSOR: SCARCELLA-SPANTON\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the penal law, in relation to the sale and possession of\nself-defense spray devices; and to repeal certain provisions of such law\nrelating thereto\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nRelates to the sale and possession of self-defense spray devices in the\nstate.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1: Amends the penal law to permit the shipping and sale of self-\ndefense spray devices within the state.\n\nSection 2. Repeals a portion of the penal law, which specified that the\n\"possession and sale of a self-defense spray device\" could only be\nperformed by a licensed firearms dealer or a licensed pharmacist who was\nauthorized and approved by the superintendent of state police.\n\nSection 3. Sets the effective date\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nSelf-defense spray devices, such as pepper spray, can be a powerful tool\nagainst attackers. With the threat of violence against women remaining a\nconcern, giving people a non-lethal tool to defend themselves from their\nwould-be attackers and escape from the situation could be life-saving.\n\nPepper spray is legal in New York state, but there are numerous\nrestrictions. Some of those restrictions prohibit shipping certain\nsprays within the state and some prohibit anyone other than a very\nnarrow group of licensed professionals from selling these devices.\nThese specific restrictions create a barrier that could prevent people\nfrom being able to defend themselves.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2024; Held in Senate Codes Committee.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nYet to be determined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
