    "lawCode": "Amd §265.20, rpld §265.20 sub a ¶15, Pen L",
    "lawSection": "Penal Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S4922C\n\nSPONSOR: SCARCELLA-SPANTON\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the penal law, in relation to the sale and possession of\nself-defense spray devices; and to repeal certain provisions of such law\nrelating thereto\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL::\n\nRelates to the sale and possession of self-defense spray devices in the\nstate.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one would amend paragraph 14 of subdivision (a) of section\n265.20 of the penal law to add a definition of \"self-defense spray,\"\npermit the use of the spray under certain justified circumstances with\nexceptions, and require that under the regulations developed by the\ndepartment of health, with the cooperation of the division of criminal\njustice services and the superintendent of state police, self-defense\nsprays shall not contain more than 1.33% by weight total capsaicinoids;\nrequire that regulations include a WARNING with specified language that\nthe spray may not be used for any purpose other than self-defense, and\nthat every spray must be accompanied by an insert with directions for\nuse, first aid, and safety and storage; Before delivery, seller must\nrequire proof of age; no more than two sprays may be sold at any one\ntime to a single purchaser.\n\nSection 2 would repeal paragraph 15 of subdivision (a) within section\n265.20 of the penal law\n\nSection three would set the effective date.\n\n \nDIFFERENCE BETWEEN A PRINT AND B PRINT:\n\nThe B print makes an amendment to require that the rules and regulations\ndeveloped by the department of health, with the cooperation of the divi-\nsion of criminal justice services and the superintendent of state police\nto include that a self-defense spray not contain more than 1.33 percent\nby weight total capsaicinoids, and this also removes the possibility of\npermitting shipping self-defense sprays within the state.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION::\n\nSelf-defense spray devices, such as pepper spray, can be a powerful tool\nagainst attackers. With the threat of violence against women remaining a\nconcern, giving people a non-lethal tool to defend themselves from their\nwould-be attackers and escape from the situation could be lifesaving.\nPepper spray is legal in New York state, but there are numerous\nrestrictions. One of these restrictions prohibits anyone other than a\nvery narrow group of licensed professionals from selling these devices.\nThese specific restrictions create a barrier that could prevent people\nfrom being able to defend themselves.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY::\n\n2023-24: referred to codes (A8140)\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS::\n\nTo be determined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE::\n\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n",
